      "text": "If $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I},$ then $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}$ is the identity matrix.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} &= \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} x + x + x &= x + x + x = 0 + 2y + (-1)z = y - z \\\\\n0 + \\frac{y y - y (-y)}{2} + \\left(\\frac{2 y (-y)-y y}{2}\\right) + \\frac{(2 y (-z) - y (-z))}{2} + \\left(\\frac{(-z)(-z-z)}{2} - \\frac{z y- z (-y)}{2}\\right) + \\frac{(-z)(-z-z)}{2} &= 0\\\\\\\\\n\\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}So $x = 0,$ $y = z.$  Then $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{y^2}.$[asy]\nsize(100);\npair A=(0,0), B=(1/sqrt(2),1/sqrt(2)), C=(1,0);\npath triangle = A--B--C--cycle;\ndraw(A--B--C--cycle);\ndot(rotate(90)*B);\ndot(rotate(90)*C);\ndraw(A--C--B);\nlabel(\"$(0,0)$\", A, SW);\nlabel(\"$(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$\", B, NE);\nlabel(\"$(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$\", C, S);\n[/asy]. The answer is \\sqrt{2},2",
