      "text": "From the problem, we have \\[\\mathbf M^T\\mathbf M = \\mathbf I.\\] The matrix $\\mathbf M^T\\mathbf M$ is equal to \\[\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & x & x \\\\\n2y & y & -y \\\\\nz & -z & z\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & 2y & z \\\\\nx & y & -z \\\\\nx & -y & z\n\\end{pmatrix}=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n2x^2+x^2+x^2 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n0+2y(2y)+y(2y) & 0+y^2+y^2 & 0+y(2y)-yz \\\\\n0 & 0 & 0+(-z)(-z)+z(-z)\n\\end{pmatrix}=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n4x^2 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n0 & 4y^2 & 0 \\\\\n0 & 0 & 2z^2\n\\end{pmatrix}\\] since $z^2(-z)=-z^3.$ Letting this matrix equal $\\mathbf I,$ we get the system \\begin{align*}\n4x^2 &= 1, \\\\\n4y^2 &= 1, \\\\\n2z^2 &= 1.\n\\end{align*}  Dividing each equation by the appropriate constants gives $x^2=\\frac{1}{4},$ $y^2=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $z^2=\\frac{1}{2}.$  Thus, $x^2+y^2+z^2=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\boxed{\\frac{5}{4}}.$ The answer is \\frac{5}{4}",
