      "text": "We begin by computing $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}.$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} &= \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + (0 - x)^2 & (0 \\cdot 2y + x \\cdot y) & (0 \\cdot z + x \\cdot z) \\\\ (2y \\cdot 0 + y \\cdot x) & (2y \\cdot 2y + y^2 + (-y)^2) & (2y \\cdot z + y \\cdot (-z) + (-y) \\cdot z) \\\\ (z \\cdot 0 + (-z) \\cdot x) & (z \\cdot 2y + (-z) \\cdot y + z \\cdot (-z)) & (z \\cdot z + (-z) \\cdot (-z) + z \\cdot z) \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + x^2 & xy & xz \\\\ xy & 5y^2 & 0 \\\\ 0& 0 & 3z^2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\nWe want this to be equal to $\\mathbf{I},$ or\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]We can begin by observing that the top-right entry is $xy,$ and the bottom-right entry is $3z^2.$  In order for the bottom-right entry to be 1, we know $zz = 1.$  Since $z^2 = 1,$ this tells us that $z = 1$ or $z = -1.$\n\nSuppose $z = -1.$  Then the bottom-right entry of $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}$ is 3.  Setting this equal to 1 tells us $3 = 1,$ which is definitely false.  So this case cannot be right.\n\nIf $z = 1,$ we see $xy = 0,$ so either $x = 0$ or $y = 0.$  Case 1.  If $x = 0,$ the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ is\n\\[\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & 1 \\\\ 0 & y & -1 \\\\ 0 & -y & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]The top-right entry is $2y^2$, and the bottom-right entry is $3-y^2.$  Setting these equal to 0 tells us $y^2 = 0,$ so $y = 0.$ But this would imply $x^2 + y^2 = 0,$ which is definitely impossible.  If $x = 0,$ this tells us $y = 0$ as well.\n\nCase 2.  If $y = 0,$ the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ is\n\\[\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2z & z \\\\ 0 & 0 & -z \\\\ 0 & 0 & z \\end{pmatrix}.\\]The top-right entry is $2z^2,$ and the bottom-right entry is $3z^2.$  Setting these equal to 0 tells us $z^2 = 0,$ which implies $z = 0.$ This means the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ is\n\\[\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]This matrix, of course, satisfies $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I}.$  In this case, however, $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 0.$\n\nThus, the answer is $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{0}.$The answer is: 0",
